## 2. Time Complexity of Common Python Operations

Python operations have different time complexities. Understanding them helps us write efficient programs.

### A. List Operations

| Operation | Time Complexity |
|---|---|
| Copy (`list.copy()`) | O(n) |
| Append (`list.append(x)`) | O(1) amortized |
| Pop last element (`list.pop()`) | O(1) |
| Pop intermediate element (`list.pop(i)`) | O(n) |
| Insert (`list.insert(i, x)`) | O(n) |
| Get item (`list[i]`) | O(1) |
| Set item (`list[i] = x`) | O(1) |
| Delete item (`del list[i]`) | O(n) |
| Iteration | O(n) |
| Get slice (`list[a:b]`) | O(k) |
| Delete slice (`del list[a:b]`) | O(n) |
| Set slice (`list[a:b] = values`) | O(n + k) |
| Extend (`list.extend(iterable)`) | O(k) amortized |
| Sort (`list.sort()`) | O(n log n) |
| Multiply (`list * k`) | O(n × k) |
| Membership (`x in list`) | O(n) |
| Length (`len(list)`) | O(1) |

Here:
- n = number of elements in the list.
- k = number of elements in the slice, iterable, or repetitions, depending on the operation.

### B. Set Operations

| Operation | Time Complexity |
|---|---|
| Membership (`x in set`) | O(1) average |

Note: Set membership can take O(n) in the worst case.

### C. Dictionary Operations

| Operation | Time Complexity |
|---|---|
| Membership (`key in dict`) | O(1) average |
| Copy (`dict.copy()`) | O(n) |
| Get item (`dict[key]`) | O(1) average |
| Set item (`dict[key] = value`) | O(1) average |
| Delete item (`del dict[key]`) | O(1) average |
| Iteration | O(n) |

Note: Dictionary operations can take O(n) in the worst case..
